      "paper_says": "The reported gender comparisons were safety F(1,43.7) = 13.89, p < 0.001; lack of changing facilities F(1,59.2) = 6.33, p = 0.015; importance of unisex toilets F(1,72.4) = 18.30, p < 0.001; and need for more women's toilets F(1,35.1) = 17.06, p < 0.001. The regression reported b = -0.920, SE = 0.281, t = -3.28, p = 0.002, beta = -0.358, and R-squared = 0.238. Observed mean use times were 121.25 seconds for men and 136.63 seconds for women overall, with peak-period means of 131 and 181.67 seconds. Thirty open-ended responses produced themes involving privacy, hygiene, commercial workarounds, care burdens, and queues, with menstruation and childcare challenges appearing exclusively in women's responses. The authors interpret these findings as evidence that formally neutral provision can produce indirect gender disadvantage and an institutional accountability gap.",
